    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d98-a1">

                <pb facs="0222" n="134" xml:id="pg134"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure in No. 754.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Leonard to Mr.
                            Wharton.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >United States Consulate</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Shanghai</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1892-09-09"><hi rendition="#italic">September 9, 1892</hi></date>.
                        (Received October 7.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 222.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to transmit herewith an
                    editorial from yesterday’s issue of the North China Daily News, published at
                    this place.</p>
                <p>Singan, the place mentioned, is more than 800 miles southwest of Peking, and
                    about 500 miles north of Ichang, the port farthest up the Yangtze River, and
                    inaccessible to any foreign force.</p>
                <p>So far as I can learn, there may be half a dozen American missionaries in that
                    region in the service of the China Inland Mission, but I can not ascertain
                    before mailing this dispatch.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">J. A. Leonard</hi>,<lb/><hi
                            rendition="#italic">Consul-General.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
